# A request goes the way its method says (#160). urllib says the method by what a request is handed: `urlopen(url)`
# without data is a `GET`, which takes what the feed holds; handed data it is a `POST`, which leaves the data there; a
# `Request(..)` it opens goes by the `method` it is built with. What `urlopen` gives is used in the body's own code
# (`.read()`, returned), so `latest` takes from the feed. No call of these bodies is a step: none names what it gives.

route feeds.latest
line entry > latest call
line Web(feeds.example.org) > latest own        # return urlopen(FEED).read()

side entry left
side Web(feeds.example.org) left

route feeds.post
line entry > post call
line post > Web(feeds.example.org) own          # urlopen(FEED, data=item.encode())

side entry left
side Web(feeds.example.org) right

route feeds.remove
line entry > remove call
line remove > Web(feeds.example.org) own        # urlopen(Request(.., method="DELETE"))

side entry left
side Web(feeds.example.org) right
